the-firm-as-one-program-lines-6 : List (List Word)
the-firm-as-one-program-lines-6 = cons "- **P1** Observation fibers: what a reading warrants, and when a participant is safe to act (§1, §10.4)." (cons "  An observation does not tell you which history happened. It tells you which histories remain compatible with what was seen: the fiber of the observation. What a reading warrants is whatever holds for every history in that fiber, and a refined observation shrinks the fiber, so a conclusion already established stays established. Nothing is guessed and no probability is introduced to hide what is missing. The same construction says when an action is safe, what a participant in a distributed system knows, and what a debugger can still say about a run it only partly saw (hyperactivity v31, §1 and §10.4)." (cons "- **P2** Programs, relations and their justifications in one language, with the reasoning kept as data (§1–2)." (cons "  A routine, the property required of it and the reasoning that establishes the property are constructions of the same language. A requirement is a chosen family of types and its proof is an inhabitant, so the reasoning an engineer already performs to build something correctly is kept for later use instead of being thrown away or rewritten in a separate verification tool (§1, §2)." (cons "- **P3** Transport of whole structures along equivalences (§3–4)." (cons "  Equality is something the language computes with. An equivalence between two representations becomes a path between them, and transport along the path carries an entire structure across at once: its operations, their laws, the programs that use them and the proofs about those programs. Changing representation stops being a rewrite and becomes an operation (§3, §4)." (cons "- **P4** Existing languages and standards as data, with adequacy proofs (§5, §8)." (cons "  A programming language is a record of syntax, judgments, configurations, steps and observations, and its meaning is one guarded unfolding. Fidelity to a published standard is an equivalence between the standard's derivations and the represented ones. C++ in full, with its distinctions between undefined, unspecified and implementation-defined behaviour, sits inside the host as data. Proofs attach to existing source as dependent pairs and erase back to the unchanged code (§5, §8)." (cons "- **P5** Specialization (the Futamura projections) that keeps its proofs (§6)." (cons "  A specializer applied to an interpreter and a program produces a residual program with the same meaning, and the three Futamura projections are typed instances of that single equation. Specialized code arrives with the path relating it to its source, so speed is obtained without giving up correctness (§6)." (cons "- **P6** Machines, operating systems and fleets as one program through composed handlers (§7)." (cons "  A processor, a kernel, its guest processes, a network and a fleet of communicating machines are processes with explicit transitions. Composing them with handlers internalizes the interactions between them, so a kernel with its guests, or an entire fleet, is a single program whose properties can be proved. Continuing processes, with their divergence, deadlock and resource bounds, have exact semantics (§7)." (cons "- **P7** One evaluator realizes everything, through C (§9)." (cons "  Compiling one evaluator, under its stated adequacy and simulation premises, gives every represented program a native realization, including the checker, the specializer and the compiler themselves. Direct compilation of each construct becomes an optimization of that universal route. The chosen route runs through C and an existing compiler, and lifting that compiler into the language turns trust in it into proof (§9)." (cons "- **P8** Dependency certificates, effect scopes and hot swapping of running code (§10.1–10.5)." (cons "  A retained result records everything it read, including what it found absent, so the system knows exactly when it is still valid. Effects carry their scope, so pure work is reused freely while a payment, a send or a write is never issued twice. A running program can be replaced while preserving the effects it has issued and the responses it is still waiting for (§10.1 to §10.5, §10.7)." (cons "- **P9** Content-addressed, decentralized exchange of code with its proofs (§10.8)." (cons "  A definition is identified by its content. A name is an alias, a change is a new object, and nothing is overwritten. Address identity, definitional equality and semantic equivalence are kept apart, and peers exchange constructions together with their proofs and dependency closures, with no global namespace and no canonical state (§10.8)." (cons "- **P10** Edits propagate through proofs, artifacts and deployment (§10.7, §10.11)." (cons "  An edit to a definition flows through everything that depends on it: the proofs, the specialized code, the native artifact, the rendering and the deployment. Old and new versions coexist, and a rollout between them is a defined transition with its invariants proved over the mixed-version interval (§10.7, §10.11)." nil)))))))))))))))))))
